Reason:
The original `check_scalar_ids()` only called `check_ids()` on the compound IDs (which include the `BPF_ADD_CONST` flag). This created an idmap entry for the flagged ID without verifying that the underlying base IDs (flag stripped) are consistent with existing mappings. Consequently, two unrelated registers with different base IDs could be incorrectly considered equivalent, allowing invalid state pruning.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (!check_ids(old_id, cur_id, idmap))
    return false;
if (old_id & BPF_ADD_CONST) {
    old_id &= ~BPF_ADD_CONST;
    cur_id &= ~BPF_ADD_CONST;
    if (!check_ids(old_id, cur_id, idmap))
        return false;
}
return true;
```
Reason:
The fix first performs the original compound ID check. If the old ID has `BPF_ADD_CONST` set, it then strips the flag from both old and current IDs and performs an additional `check_ids()` on the base IDs. This ensures that if the compound ID mapped, the underlying base IDs must also map consistently (e.g., if base A was already mapped to B, then a new compound mapping must have base B, not an unrelated C). This prevents the construction of mismatched base-ID mappings that would bypass the state pruning checks.

